4.2  PRE-COMMIT FUNCTIONALITY VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Before EVERY commit, verify the code actually works. Commits are
blocked if any of these fail:

  PRE-COMMIT GATE (automated - blocks the commit):
    [ ] All unit tests pass (pytest, vitest)
    [ ] All linting clean (Black, Ruff, ESLint, Prettier)
    [ ] All type checks clean (mypy on backend, tsc on frontend)
    [ ] All migrations apply cleanly (alembic upgrade head, then
        alembic downgrade -1, then alembic upgrade head again)
    [ ] No secrets in the diff (gitleaks pre-commit hook)
    [ ] Build succeeds (vite build, docker build)
    [ ] OWASP automated scans from §4.1 all clean

  PRE-PUSH GATE (automated - blocks the push):
    [ ] All pre-commit gates pass
    [ ] All integration tests pass
    [ ] All E2E critical-path tests pass (signup, intake, run
        analysis, release deliverable, message send)
    [ ] Accessibility checks pass on touched pages (axe-core)

  PRE-MERGE GATE (automated - the merge to main blocks):
    [ ] All pre-push gates pass
    [ ] PR description references the spec section implemented
    [ ] Manual smoke-test steps documented in PR description
    [ ] OWASP manual review checklist from §4.1 completed in the
        PR description

  PHASE-COMPLETE CHECKPOINT (logged, not blocking - just logged):
    [ ] All acceptance criteria for the phase from spec §15 PASS
    [ ] CHANGELOG.md updated
    [ ] Documentation in docs/ updated for any new feature
    [ ] Phase-complete entry written to DECISIONS.md
    [ ] Continue to next phase immediately

Configure pre-commit hooks (use the pre-commit framework) and CI to
enforce all of these automatically. If a hook is bypassed via
--no-verify the commit message MUST include "BYPASS: <reason>" and
the very next commit must fix the underlying issue. Bypasses are
visible in DECISIONS.md.

"It compiles" is NOT verification. "The test runs" is NOT verification.
Verification means: the new feature does what the spec says it should
do, exercised end-to-end with a real request that produces the
expected response or side effect. Document the smoke test in the
commit message body when it's a notable feature.

Commit hygiene:
  - One logical unit per commit. Multiple commits per PR is fine; one
    massive commit is not.
  - Conventional Commits format.
  - Commit message references the spec section being implemented
    (e.g., "feat(intake): add 'I'm not sure' option per spec §6.2").
  - Never commit broken code with the intent to fix it next commit.
    If the work is mid-stream, push to a feature branch and do not
    open the PR until it works.

