6. DECISIONS AND FLAGGING - HOW TO PROCEED AUTONOMOUSLY
================================================================================

You never stop to ask. You always proceed. But you keep an audit
trail of every meaningful decision in DECISIONS.md at the repo root.

For every decision that is NOT explicit in the spec, write one entry
in DECISIONS.md using this format:

    ## D-<NNN> [<DATE>] <Short title>
    Context:        <one sentence>
    Decision:       <what you chose>
    Alternatives:   <option A, option B, option C>
    Why:            <one paragraph rationale>
    Reversibility:  <Easy / Medium / Hard>
    Eugene review:  <Recommended / Optional>

Examples of decisions that should be logged:
  - LLM provider choice (Anthropic Claude vs. OpenAI)
  - PDF library choice (WeasyPrint vs. ReportLab)
  - Charting library choice (Recharts vs. Tremor)
  - Authentication library specifics
  - Any flip on the seven open questions in spec section 17
  - Any place you deviated from the master spec because of a
    technical constraint
  - Any place you added a feature the spec didn't call for but
    that seemed obviously required (log it explicitly)

Examples of decisions that do NOT need logging:
  - Variable naming
  - File naming within a feature
  - Test file organization
  - Code formatting
  - "Use shadcn default for this control"

DECISIONS.md is sectioned by phase. At the end of the build, it is
Eugene's primary review surface for understanding what you decided
and why. Keep it concise but complete.

If you discover a contradiction in the spec, log it as a decision
that picks the more conservative interpretation and continue.

If you discover the spec is impossible (e.g., library X that the
spec assumes doesn't exist), log it as a decision that substitutes
the nearest equivalent and continue. Make the substitution
documented and reversible.

